<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Hungary (1526-1867)</h1><p class="st-meta">1526 CE – 1867 CE · capital Pressburg; later Buda</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Habsburg-ruled Kingdom of Hungary, from the narrow frontier strip of Royal Hungary to the reconquered kingdom that won self-government in 1867.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-hungary">Louis II</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs">Mohács</a> in 1526, a diet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pozsony</a> elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-holy-roman-emperor">Ferdinand of Habsburg</a> king, against the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-zapolya">John Zápolya</a>. The Ottoman capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> in 1541 left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> with only a western and northern strip, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a>, governed from Pressburg, where diets met and kings were crowned.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> kept its own diet, counties and noble privileges while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> controlled war and foreign affairs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> was the official language until 1844. Ottoman wars dominated until the reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a> in 1686 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699. Revolts by Bocskai, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Thököly</a> and Rákóczi defended Protestant and noble rights; the Reform Era brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istvan-szechenyi">Széchenyi</a>'s modernisation.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The revolution of 1848 and the war of independence were crushed in 1849 with Russian help, followed by direct rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1866 forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-compromise-of-1867">Compromise of 1867</a>, which created <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and the self-governing <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary</a> (1867-1918).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-hungary-1526-1867.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Hungary (1526-1867)" width="320" height="305"><figcaption>Image: WikiEditor2004 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_europe_1572.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1526 CE – 1867 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pressburg; later Buda</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ferdinand I, Maria Theresa, Franz Joseph I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic and Protestant Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hungarian, Latin, German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Hungary, Slovakia, Croatia, Romania, Serbia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Royal Hungary, Habsburg Hungary</dd></dl></aside></div>
